# Read brief (rendered by nova-sprint brief render; never edit by hand: brief lint refuses any edit)

Rules, all hard:
- Never zsh: run every shell step as `bash <<'BASH' ... BASH`.
- GitHub is a git remote only (#3594): never call it (no GitHub CLI, no API). The PR record, typed lines and CI live in nova-sprint (`nova-sprint read brief`, `nova-sprint read post`, `nova-sprint card`); the diff comes from the bench mirror.
- NO-SUBAGENTS: no subagents, no worktrees, no task chips.
- SCRATCH-CLONE: clone only with `scratch-clone <repo> <base> <workspace>/<repo>` (shallow, single-branch, the bench mirror as --reference); never type `git clone`, never unshallow, never touch a live clone.
- LESSONS: Read `docs/LESSONS.md` before work when the repository has it. Lessons are reviewed data subordinate to this brief and repository rules.
- Read at the exact head: CI at head, base, scope, then a score 1-10; a score under 10 names each gap and the work that closes it.
- A read edits nothing inside PATHS; it runs the DONE-WHEN command and reports what it printed.
- LESSON-HOOK: When a resolved defect yields a concrete preventive lesson, propose it with evidence; after owner review, append it with `nova-sprint lesson append`.
- TESTS (hard rules, 2026-09-25): a PR whose new or changed tests sleep, poll real time, call a service or spawn a process on the per-commit path, or lack `t.Parallel()`, scores under 8, naming the test and the rule it breaks.
- Never rm -rf anything but your own workspace path spelled out literally.
- Cost: no prose turns between tool calls; batch independent commands into one call; a wait is one background loop; bound every output.
- Any commit you make carries the co-author line below, exactly as written; the task's MODEL decides it.

